{
  "step_1": "The decision reason involves adjusting the probabilities of outcomes with less than 20% probability by increasing them by 10 percentage points (i.e., adding 0.10 to the probability), then computing a weighted sum of outcomes using these adjusted probabilities. This adjusted expected value is used to compare lotteries. In this decision problem, both lotteries have outcomes with probabilities below 20%: Lottery A has a 100% probability (which is above 20%), so it is not adjusted; Lottery B has a 0.1% (0.001) probability of winning 5000 Euros, which is below 20%, so it would be adjusted to 10.1% (0.101). The 99.9% outcome (0 Euros) in Lottery B is also below 20% and would be adjusted to 109.9% (0.1099), but since probabilities must sum to 100%, the adjustment rule must be applied carefully. However, the reason specifies that outcomes with probability <20% receive an increased weight by 10 percentage points, which implies a transformation of individual probabilities, but it does not specify whether the adjustment is applied independently or normalized. Given the ambiguity, the most plausible interpretation is that only the low-probability outcomes are overweighted by 10 percentage points, and the total probability remains 100%, so the adjustment likely involves redistributing the 10 percentage points across the low-probability outcomes. However, since only the 0.1% outcome is below 20%, and the 99.9% outcome is also below 20% (as 99.9% > 20%? No — 99.9% is above 20%, so only the 0.1% outcome is below 20%). Wait: 0.1% < 20%, so only the 5000 Euro outcome qualifies. The 0 Euro outcome has 99.9% probability, which is greater than 20%, so it is not adjusted. Thus, only the 0.1% probability is increased by 10 percentage points (i.e., to 10.1%), and the 99.9% probability is reduced to 89.9% to maintain total probability. Then, the weighted sum for Lottery B becomes: 5000 * 0.101 + 0 * 0.899 = 50.5. For Lottery A: 5 * 1 = 5. So the adjusted sum for B (50.5) is hig